Stop the pending auto-claim from silently merging unrelated transactions.

Account::ProviderImportAdapter#find_pending_transaction reconciles pending→posted
by matching amount + currency inside an 8-day window, then takes
.order(date: :desc).first. It compares neither merchant nor name, and the claim is
destructive: the posted transaction is welded onto the pending entry, which keeps
its date and merchant. Two same-amount transactions a few days apart are therefore
silently merged into one row that belongs to neither.

Observed in production: a 52 EUR charge on the 21st claimed an unrelated 52 EUR
pending row from the 14th, producing an entry with the first merchant's name and
the second's date and merchant_id.

The risk model is inverted. The two paths that only ever *suggest* a match
(find_pending_transaction_fuzzy, find_pending_transaction_low_confidence) both use
a tighter window, require merchant or name evidence, and refuse when ambiguous.
The one path that rewrites data has the widest window and no identity check at all.

Adds two guards to the destructive path, and deliberately does NOT require positive
identity agreement:

  1. Ambiguity — refuse when more than one candidate matches, rather than picking
     the most recent on an amount match alone.
  2. Merchant contradiction — refuse when both sides resolved a merchant and those
     merchants disagree. A negative test, so it stays inert where the pending row
     carries no merchant.

Requiring positive merchant/name agreement was considered and rejected: providers
legitimately format pending and booked descriptions differently (Enable Banking
sends "PAIEMENT CARTE DU 20/08 15:14 MAISON DE OC" then "CARTE X0571 20/08 MAISON
DE OCEAN"), which is why this method does not compare names. Audited against 61
production auto-claims on such a provider, all legitimate, none of which either
guard rejects.

Narrowing date_window was also considered and rejected as an arbitrary threshold
that moves the boundary without addressing the missing identity check.

